```java
public class MonotonicIdGenerator {
    private final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);

    public long nextId() {
        return counter.incrementAndGet();
    }
}
```

An atomic long gives thread-safe, monotonically increasing IDs with a single instruction on modern hardware — no explicit lock needed, since the increment-and-get operation is itself atomic.

```java
public class UuidIdGenerator {
    public String nextId() {
        return UUID.randomUUID().toString();
    }
}
```

No shared counter state at all — any number of threads, or even separate processes with zero coordination, can call this and get IDs that won't collide in practice. The trade: the result carries no ordering information whatsoever; two IDs generated a millisecond apart sort in effectively random order.
